## How to Prioritize by Actionability When AI Gives You Too Much Information

If you're the only marketer on a small team, you know this one.

You get handed a brief that was written by AI. Nobody read it all the way through. So before you can do any work, you have to figure out what the person actually needs.

Then you open AI yourself to research it, and everything it gives you looks useful. By the end of the day you have a bigger pile and no decision.

I heard this line on a webinar recently and it stuck with me:

***“Content without execution is just entertainment.”***

Most of what we read about marketing and AI is like that. It stays theory until you test it. So here is the experiment I ran on my own week.

## **The problem**

Two things kept happening.

1. **Unclear briefs.** Whenever I started a new project, my senior would hand me AI-generated instructions he hadn't fully read. I'd spend time working out what he actually needed before I could start.
1. **Research that never ends.** When I used AI to research, everything I thought might be important or useful piled up. I hit decision paralysis, and tasks took much longer than they should have.

**[REAL DETAIL: one specific example of each, kept generic. How long did a task take versus how long it should have?]**

## **What I wanted**

Two things. A team that pulls in the same direction each week, and a way to finish a task without losing a day to choosing what to look at.

## **How I figured it out**

I stopped trying to fix the information problem and fixed the priorities problem instead.

I proposed a weekly meeting on Monday morning. Everyone sets their own three priorities for the week, all tied to one main team goal. On Friday, we report what is in progress, what is done, and what needs recalibrating before the next Monday.

Our first goal was to get our content marketing loop working. My part was making sure our AI-generated content looked good on every platform we were targeting.

I didn't start with every platform. In week one, I only worked on the blog structure and SEO. That gave me three priorities:

- Technical SEO optimization
- SEO content
- Content pillars

Before starting, I decided what I'd measure:

- Are we showing up on Google for our brand terms?
- Is the site healthy?
- Are the quick fixes getting any traction?

Week two was LinkedIn setup. It was faster and easier, because the week-one groundwork was already done. Focusing on one thing at a time beat spreading effort across every platform.

## **The system**

1. **Monday: pick three.** Each person chooses three priorities that serve one shared goal for the week.
1. **Focus on one area at a time.** Finish the foundation before you spread to the next platform.
1. **Decide the measures up front.** Know what you'll check on Friday before you start on Monday.
1. **Friday: report.** What is in progress, what is done, what needs recalibrating.
1. **Start the next Monday from Friday's recalibration.** The loop is: Decide → Act → Measure → Learn → Refine → Repeat.

The rule behind it:

***Don't prioritize by importance. Prioritize by actionability.***

A task sitting on a to-do list isn't progress. If it has no clear next action, no owner and no time to do it, it quietly disappears. Importance tells you what matters. Actionability tells you what will happen this week.

Sometimes the right move is to spend an hour sorting the mess out. Not because organizing feels productive, but because clarity makes execution easier.

### **Steal this: the Monday template**

- **Team goal this week:** (one sentence)
- **My three priorities:** 1. ___ 2. ___ 3. ___
- **What I'll measure:** (two or three things I can check on Friday)

And before anything makes the list, run it through this actionability check:

- What is the very next action?
- Who does it? ("Someone" means it isn't ready.)
- When exactly will it happen?
- How will I know it worked?

If any answer is missing, fix it in 10 minutes or take it off the list.

## **The AI part: making the output consistent**

The same rhythm helped with the AI side too. Because this isn't only a content problem. It's a problem of managing information and AI itself.

To get consistent content, I worked with AI to write master prompts. I built them from experience and from what I'd learned from experts I follow. Then I worked with our developer to make sure the agents ran properly.

It was a lot of back and forth: regenerating blog posts until the output matched what we wanted.

AI also forgets. So we ran the agents every day for a week. Wherever the output wasn't consistent, we tightened the guardrails again. That way future posts would always follow the correct structure, and would reference the correct performance data when the system improves itself.

The lesson: you don't build an agent and walk away. You give it context, test the output, correct it, and refine it. Only then do you supervise it less.

**[REAL DETAIL: one example of a guardrail you added after an inconsistent output, written generically]**

## **The result**

Week two was noticeably faster and easier than week one, because the foundation was in place and we had one shared focus instead of ten.

**[REAL RESULT: brand-term appearances on Google, before → after]**

